# PayBridge webapp — environment variables
#
# ⚠ READ THIS FIRST — THERE ARE NO SECRETS IN THIS FILE, EVER.
#
# Vite inlines every variable prefixed with VITE_ into the JavaScript bundle at
# build time. That bundle is downloaded by every visitor and is trivially
# readable — "view source" is the whole attack. An API key, a database URL or a
# signing secret placed here is a PUBLISHED credential, not a configured one.
# Anything that must stay private belongs in the backend's environment, behind
# an API route the browser calls.
#
# Copy to `.env` for local development. `.env` and `.env.*` are gitignored;
# only this example is tracked.

# Backend origin.
#
# PRODUCTION: leave BLANK. The app then uses relative URLs (/api/...), which
# means requests are same-origin. WHY that is the secure default: same-origin
# requests need no CORS relaxation, cookies flow without SameSite=None, and
# there is no second origin to keep in the allowlist. This works even though
# the backend is deployed separately on Render, because vercel.json rewrites
# /api/* to the Render URL — the browser only ever sees the Vercel domain.
#
# DEVELOPMENT: point at the backend when it runs on another port.
# Example: VITE_BACKEND_URL=http://localhost:3000
VITE_BACKEND_URL=

# Enables the one-click demo sign-in buttons.
#
# WHY this is gated: the demo accounts bypass credential entry and can land you
# in an employer or operations portal. That is a useful showcase and an
# unacceptable production authentication bypass. Demo login is ON automatically
# in development and OFF in a production build unless this is explicitly set to
# "true". Do not set it on a real deployment.
VITE_ENABLE_DEMO_LOGIN=
